---
/**
 * How it works — the pipeline in depth.
 *
 * Copy derives from docs/marketing/product-narrative.md §3 and §1.4. Tense discipline is
 * load-bearing here: the autonomous lane is LIVE and written in the present tense; removing the
 * approval step for bank-matched payments is NEXT and is labelled as such, never implied as done.
 */
import CTASection from '@/components/CTASection.astro'
import PageHero from '@/components/PageHero.astro'
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const steps = [
  {
    n: '01',
    name: 'Ingest',
    line: 'Read once, never twice',
    body: 'Verity polls your bank feed every few minutes and resumes from a saved cursor rather than re-sweeping history. Ingestion is idempotent: a retried poll, a network blip, or a restart that lands mid-batch produces no duplicate rows.',
    detail:
      'This is the least glamorous step and the one most in-house builds get wrong. Exactly-once intake is what makes every downstream decision safe to repeat.',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    name: 'Match',
    line: 'Matched only on proof',
    body: 'Each transaction is matched to the invoice it belongs to — on confirmed evidence, or not at all. An attribution-grade reference that the payment itself carries, confirmed by fetching that invoice from your billing system, clears the floor. Nothing else does.',
    detail:
      'Zero candidates is an exception. More than one candidate is an exception. An unresolved sibling transaction against the same invoice in the same batch is an exception. The system does not break ties.',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    name: 'Classify',
    line: 'Duplicate, overpayment, or clean',
    body: 'Matched transactions resolve to one of four types. Duplicate detection reads Verity’s own confirmed history — never your billing system’s opaque “paid” flag, which can be set for a dozen reasons Verity cannot see.',
    detail:
      'Reasoning about money from a flag you did not write and cannot explain is how wrong credits get issued. We read what we can prove.',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    name: 'Act',
    line: 'A credit where one is owed',
    body: 'Duplicates and overpayments produce a credit note for the right amount. Normal payments are reconciled with no credit at all. The decision is recorded as an automated decision, in its own record, labelled as automation.',
    detail:
      'It is never written into the human decision record. No report can later present it as something a person signed off.',
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    name: 'Post',
    line: 'Written back, idempotently',
    body: 'The correction is queued and drained to your billing system by a separate worker, with idempotency and in-doubt recovery. A write that fails is retried without risking a second posting.',
    detail:
      'The dangerous case is not a failed write — it is a write that succeeded while the acknowledgement was lost. That is handled as its own state rather than retried blindly.',
  },
  {
    n: '06',
    name: 'Report',
    line: 'Posted back to your books',
    body: 'At the end of the day you get two lists: everything the pipeline settled, each with the basis for its decision, and the short list of items whose evidence did not settle.',
    detail:
      'The two cannot overlap. An item is either something the pipeline resolved or something waiting for you — never presented as both.',
  },
]

const types = [
  { type: 'normal_payment', means: 'The payment settles the invoice it names', does: 'Reconciled. No credit.', tone: 'confirmed' },
  { type: 'duplicate', means: 'The invoice was already settled by an earlier confirmed payment', does: 'Credit note for the duplicated amount', tone: 'confirmed' },
  { type: 'overpayment', means: 'The payment exceeds what the invoice was for', does: 'Credit note for the excess', tone: 'confirmed' },
  { type: 'unmatchable', means: 'The evidence does not settle it', does: 'Routed to a person, with the reasoning attached', tone: 'exception' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How it works"
  description="Verity polls your bank feed, matches each payment to an invoice on confirmed evidence, classifies it, credits what is owed, and posts it back to your billing system. Anything ambiguous becomes an exception with the reasoning attached."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="How it works"
    title="What happens between a payment landing and your books being right."
    lede="Verity sits between two systems you already have: your bank, and your billing system. Between them it does the loop a person is doing today — six steps, of which five need no one."
  />

  {/* --------------------------------------------------------------- the two doors */}
  <section class="section-y">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="border-hairline bg-surface rounded-lg border p-8">
          <span class="chip chip-confirmed">primary</span>
          <h2 class="mt-5 text-2xl font-medium">The bank feed finds the work</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-muted mt-4 leading-relaxed">
            Nobody types anything. Payments arrive, Verity works out what each one was, and the ones
            it can prove are handled end to end — including the write-back. This is the engine, and
            it runs whether or not anyone signs in.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="border-hairline bg-surface rounded-lg border p-8">
          <span class="chip chip-neutral">secondary</span>
          <h2 class="mt-5 text-2xl font-medium">A person asks, in plain language</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-muted mt-4 leading-relaxed">
            “Credit $2,000 on INV-1042 — duplicate charge.” The agent extracts the invoice, amount
            and reason, fetches the invoice, and checks it against policy before any draft exists.
            It is a real feature and it demos well, but it is the fallback — not the engine.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- the six steps */}
  <section class="rule-top section-y">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="label">The pipeline</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-[clamp(1.8rem,3.2vw,2.6rem)] leading-[1.1] font-medium">
        Six steps, in order, every few minutes.
      </h2>

      <ol class="mt-16 space-y-px">
        {
          steps.map((step) => (
            <li class="border-hairline reveal grid gap-6 border-t py-10 md:grid-cols-[7rem_1fr_1fr] md:gap-10">
              <div>
                <p class="mono text-accent text-xs">{step.n}</p>
                <h3 class="mt-2 text-xl font-medium">{step.name}</h3>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed">{step.body}</p>
              </div>
              <div class="md:pt-1">
                <p class="mono text-ink-subtle text-xs tracking-wide uppercase">{step.line}</p>
                <p class="text-ink-muted mt-3 leading-relaxed">{step.detail}</p>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ the matching floor */}
  <section class="rule-top section-y">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="grid gap-14 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1fr]">
        <div>
          <p class="label">The matching floor</p>
          <h2 class="mt-4 text-[clamp(1.8rem,3.2vw,2.6rem)] leading-[1.1] font-medium">
            The model advises. It never authorizes.
          </h2>
          <div class="text-ink-muted mt-7 space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Bank narration is hostile to machines. <span class="mono text-ink text-base">WIRE
              IN NORTHWIND TRADING INV-1042</span> is a good day; a reference field containing
              nothing but a trace number is a normal one.
            </p>
            <p>
              A language model reads that narration and proposes a candidate. That proposal is
              <span class="text-ink font-medium"> advisory</span>: it can enrich an exception so the
              person reviewing it starts with context, and it cannot be the thing that causes money
              to move.
            </p>
            <p>
              What can: a reference the payment itself carries, confirmed against the invoice it
              names. Everything above that floor is handled automatically. Everything below it
              becomes an exception.
            </p>
            <p class="text-ink">
              This is the distinction the whole product rests on — <span class="font-medium"
                >existence is not attribution</span
              >. Finding a number that looks like an invoice reference is not the same as knowing
              the payment belongs to that invoice.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <div class="border-hairline bg-surface rounded-lg border p-6 sm:p-8">
            <p class="label">Classification</p>
            <ul class="mt-6 space-y-6">
              {
                types.map((c) => (
                  <li class="border-hairline grid gap-2 border-b pb-6 last:border-0 last:pb-0 sm:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] sm:gap-5">
                    <span class:list={['chip', c.tone === 'confirmed' ? 'chip-confirmed' : 'chip-exception']}>
                      {c.type}
                    </span>
                    <div>
                      <p class="text-sm">{c.means}</p>
                      <p class="text-ink-muted mt-1 text-sm">{c.does}</p>
                    </div>
                  </li>
                ))
              }
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p class="text-ink-subtle mt-5 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Ambiguity resolves to <span class="mono">unmatchable</span> — never to a guess. A tie is
            not broken; it is escalated.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the day, and next */}
  <section class="rule-top section-y">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="grid gap-14 lg:grid-cols-[1.1fr_0.9fr]">
        <div>
          <p class="label">Oversight</p>
          <h2 class="mt-4 text-[clamp(1.8rem,3.2vw,2.6rem)] leading-[1.1] font-medium">
            Your 5:30pm, not your 9am.
          </h2>
          <div class="text-ink-muted mt-7 space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Because every action is stamped at write time with what kind of actor took it, Verity
              can answer a question most finance tools cannot: what fraction of your money decisions
              had no human in the loop.
            </p>
            <p>
              Most systems cannot tell their own automation apart from their users. That makes
              “the system did this automatically” an awkward discovery in an audit rather than a
              defensible answer. Here it is a first-class, recorded fact.
            </p>
            <p>
              Verity also watches for the failure nobody notices: a feed that is configured but not
              being processed. An empty queue looks like a quiet week right up until close.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="border-hairline bg-surface rounded-lg border p-8">
          <span class="chip chip-neutral">shipping next</span>
          <h3 class="mt-5 text-xl font-medium">
            Removing the approval step for bank-matched payments
          </h3>
          <p class="text-ink-muted mt-4 leading-relaxed">
            Where attribution is confirmed, the pipeline will classify, credit and post with no
            pre-write human gate. The finance head’s role becomes the end-of-day report and the
            exception queue.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-muted mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            It would be reckless if approval were the only control. It isn’t, and it was never the
            strongest one — a person rubber-stamping a queue of confident matches is approving the
            same evidence the system already confirmed. The matching floor, idempotent writes, the
            recoverable posting queue, honest audit and the exception route all stay exactly where
            they are. Discretionary credits keep a human decision, because there the evidence is a
            commercial judgement rather than a fact about a payment.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection />
</BaseLayout>
